# Reproducibility & Release Rules

Distilled from: NeurIPS 2026 Call for Reproducibility, NeurIPS Code Submission Policy (Papers with Code "releasing-research-code" guidelines), NeurIPS 2026 Evaluations & Datasets Track requirements (Croissant metadata), ML Reproducibility Checklist (Pineau et al.).

## 1. What must be releasable

If the paper makes empirical claims, the following must be releasable (or you must justify why not in Limitations / Appendix):

- Inference code (load weights → take wav → produce transcript)
- Training code (or a faithful reference implementation; close-source justification ok)
- Eval scripts that reproduce every number in the main results table
- Configs (model, training, eval) as version-controlled YAML / JSON, not just buried in shell commands
- Model weights (Hugging Face Hub or equivalent persistent host)
- A `requirements.txt` or `pyproject.toml` pinned to specific versions; for CUDA-sensitive deps, document the exact CUDA / PyTorch combination
- A README in the release repo that walks through reproducing the headline LibriSpeech (or equivalent) result end-to-end

## 2. Dataset release standard

For any in-house data we release alongside the model:

- Hosted on a long-term-preservation platform (Hugging Face Hub, Harvard Dataverse, Zenodo). Persistent identifier (DOI or HF dataset URL).
- Croissant `metadata.json` with both core and Responsible-AI (RAI) fields.
- License: explicit SPDX identifier. Note any access restrictions.
- A datasheet (see Gebru et al. "Datasheets for Datasets") covering: motivation, composition, collection process, preprocessing, uses, distribution, maintenance.
- PII / consent statement: what was scrubbed, what was not, what consent the speakers gave.

## 3. Model card

We MUST ship a model card with the released weights. Required sections:

- Intended use and out-of-scope use
- Training data summary (per-source hours, language coverage, time period)
- Evaluation: WER on each main benchmark with normalization spec (see `asr-domain.mdc`)
- Known limitations (per Limitations section of the paper)
- Bias and fairness: per-accent / per-dialect WER breakdown if available
- Carbon footprint: training compute (GPU-hours) and approximate kWh / CO₂e
- Contact and licensing

## 4. LLM use disclosure (ICLR / NeurIPS 2026)

- Maintain `ack/llm-usage.md` (create if missing). Each entry: date, scope (e.g. "drafted §3.2 introduction sentence"), model used.
- Acknowledgment section in the paper must state "Portions of the manuscript were drafted with the assistance of large language models. All technical content was verified by the authors."
- Never paste a citation produced solely by an LLM without verifying the entry exists (author / year / venue) on a primary source.

## 5. Ethics and broader impact

- The paper must include a Limitations section (already in `sections/08_limitations_ethics.tex`). Be specific, not boilerplate. Examples to consider:
  - Surveillance and unconsented transcription risk
  - Accent / dialect bias (especially if the data skews mainstream Mandarin or American English)
  - Hallucination risk in medical / legal settings
  - Overlapping speech and diarization is out of scope
- Carbon footprint: report training-time GPU-hours and (where available) PUE-adjusted estimate.
- Data provenance: do not include data without verified license. If any source is borderline, document why we believe inclusion is acceptable (e.g. "publicly broadcast under CC-BY", "explicit speaker consent recorded in the source").

## 6. Reproducibility checklist (run before any submission / public release)

1. `git status` clean and tagged
2. `latexmk -pdf main.tex` produces PDF with zero warnings (or warnings annotated in CHANGELOG)
3. Every cite in the rendered PDF resolves (no `[?]` markers)
4. Every `% TODO` removed or moved to a tracked issue
5. Numbers in main tables traceable to a script in the release repo
6. Model card and datasheet drafted
7. `ack/llm-usage.md` reviewed and reflected in Acknowledgments
8. `requirements.txt` produces a working environment from a fresh venv on the documented Python / CUDA versions
